# Congress
Temporary seated Congress: goes in session and then out of session and then in session and... When Congress is out of session, it is encouraged the Congresspeople go back to their place.



# Presidency
The president must be a natural born citizen. This means born on American territory or American parents must immediately assert their child's citizenship.

Constitutional authority describes powers from the Constitution, while statutory authority are powers from laws.

Take care clause: must execute laws to the best of their ability



# Bureaucracy
Serves to help the president in their duties (there's so much for one person).

Red tape describes excessive or cumbersome rules. 

Bureaucracy typically operates under fire alarm oversight—only act when the "alarm is pulled" (e.g., bridge collapsed). This is more cost effective, albeit immediately more damming, than police patrol oversight, which is constantly monitoring the situation and fixing the tiny problems that emerge.



# Judiciary
In the beginning, the Constitution gave the Supreme Court surface-level power. Supreme Court Chief Justice John Marshall changed that by establishing judicial review using the Necessary and Proper clause. 



# Public Opinion and Media
Policy mood are the issues people are actively concerned about and how much they want the government to step up to address it.

Ideological polarization describes a developing hostility as more people become less moderate and more conservative or liberal.

Even if its disliked, a viewer will still perceive the station's messages negatively, even when the message was neutral. 



# Political Parties
Unified government is where Congress and the president are of the same political party. Divided government is where this is not true. There's a belief that divided government is more functional because politicians expect to negotiate. Whereas in unified government, they are caught off guard when they need to negotiate.

Ranked choice has someone rank the candidates. If someone is ranked first by 50% of people, they are the candidate. If not, the person ranked first by the least amount of people is pulled off and second place becomes first. Keep going until the condition is met.



# Interest Groups
Disturbance theory is that groups form to counteract changes in policy that end up threatening a segment of the population (e.g., abortion).

Government can be pluralist, meaning anyone can and will access government. Elitism is where government only really pays attention to the corporations. In this U.S., this tends to vary from issue to issue.




# Elections
When people started moving west, states offered universal white male suffrage. This made the voter base less wealthy too.

The 23rd Amendment gave the District of Columbia (D.C.) electoral college votes. The 26th Amendment brought the age requirement down from 21 to 18.

Most elections are plurality, meaning the winner just has to get the most votes. Majority requires 50% + 1. Depending on the implementation, a runoff election will occur if no candidate meets this threshold. This will pit the top two candidates against each other.

Voting Available Population (VAP) is the covered people, including non-citizens. Voting Eligible Population (VEP) are only people who can vote. VAP is sometimes used in formula grants and the House of Representatives.

Uncivilized Native Americans lived on reservations. Civilized Native Americans lived in cities and such.